course: dictionary
lesson: 4941
title: Impossibly and its two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Impossibly» is an adverb, so it tells you how something is or how something happens. It has two meanings. The first is in a way that cannot happen or be done. The second, and more common in stories, is extremely, so much that it seems impossible: «impossibly tall» means so tall it seems impossible.
explain: To choose the right meaning, read the clues. «Many players who seem impossibly good have simply practised for a very long time.» Practice takes time, so impossibly good here means extremely good, not truly impossible. Try the swap test: put «very, very» into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Be careful with maths and science. Sometimes a sentence really is about something that cannot happen. «Saturn is less dense than water, so in an impossibly huge bath it would float.» The bath is imagined, so impossibly means a bath too big to exist, but the main idea is that Saturn is so light for its size that it would float.
fact: «Impossibly» means in a way that cannot happen or be done, or extremely, so much that it seems impossible.
fact: Most of the time in stories it just means very, very: «impossibly hard», «impossibly beautiful», «impossibly fast».
fact: «Impossibly» is an adverb, so it describes how something is, like «impossibly tall».
fact: Saturn is a giant ball of gas, about nine times wider than the Earth, and so light for its size that it would float in an impossibly huge bath.
fact: A quick check is to swap in «extremely»: if the sentence still makes sense, that is probably the meaning you need.
exercise: «Many players who seem impossibly good have simply practised for a very long time.» What does impossibly mean here? | In a way that cannot happen or be done | *Extremely, so good it seems impossible | Badly and carelessly | Quietly and slowly
exercise: «An impossibly huge bath» is a bath so big it seems impossible. Which word is the closest swap for impossibly? | Slowly | *Extremely | Quietly | Tiny
exercise: «Saturn is less dense than water, so in an impossibly huge bath it would float.» Why would Saturn float? | The bath is empty | It is painted bright yellow | *It is less dense than water | It is made of metal
test: Which phrase uses impossibly to mean extremely? | Impossibly, the pencil is cold | Impossibly, the sun is cold | Impossibly, the moon is made of cheese | *«impossibly fast»
test: Lena says impossibly always means never. What is the best reply? | Yes, it always means never | *No, it often means very, very, as in «impossibly hard» | No, it means very small | Yes, but only in maths
exam: What does «impossibly beautiful» mean in a story? | *Extremely beautiful, so it seems impossible | In a way that cannot be done | Dull and plain | Beautifully and quietly
